# A Tour of Ethers

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:ethers, "~> 1.0"},
    {:ex_secp256k1, "~> 0.7"}
  ],
  config: [ethereumex: [url: "https://ethereum-rpc.publicnode.com"]]
)
```

## Before you start

This notebook reads from Ethereum mainnet through a public RPC endpoint. Nothing in it sends a
transaction or needs funds. To use another endpoint or another chain, change the URL in the setup
cell above.

## Read from a token

Ethers ships with the interfaces of the common token standards. `Ethers.Contracts.ERC20` works
with any ERC-20 token, so all it needs is the address of one. This is WETH:

```elixir
alias Ethers.Contracts.ERC20

weth = "0xC02aaA39b223FE8D0A0e5C4F27eAD9083C756Cc2"

ERC20.symbol() |> Ethers.call!(to: weth)
```

Contract functions do not touch the network. They return a value that describes the call:

```elixir
ERC20.total_supply()
```

`Ethers.call!/2` executes it and decodes the result. Token amounts are integers in the smallest
unit, and `Ethers.Utils.from_wei/1` converts an 18-decimal amount for display.

```elixir
ERC20.total_supply() |> Ethers.call!(to: weth) |> Ethers.Utils.from_wei()
```

## Resolve a name

`Ethers.NameService` resolves ENS names to addresses.

```elixir
vitalik = Ethers.NameService.resolve!("vitalik.eth")
```

```elixir
ERC20.balance_of(vitalik) |> Ethers.call!(to: weth) |> Ethers.Utils.from_wei()
```

## Many calls at once

`Ethers.Multicall` bundles calls into a single request. Every result comes from the same block and
is returned as a `{success?, value}` tuple.

```elixir
calls = [
  {ERC20.name(), to: weth},
  {ERC20.symbol(), to: weth},
  {ERC20.decimals(), to: weth},
  {ERC20.balance_of(vitalik), to: weth}
]

calls
|> Ethers.Multicall.aggregate3()
|> Ethers.call!()
|> Ethers.Multicall.decode(calls)
```

## Generate a module from an ABI

WETH has functions that are not part of ERC-20: `deposit` wraps ether and `withdraw` unwraps it.
For your own contracts you give `Ethers.Contract` the ABI, usually with `abi_file: "path/to.json"`.
Here a small part of the WETH ABI is passed inline.

```elixir
defmodule WETH do
  use Ethers.Contract,
    default_address: "0xC02aaA39b223FE8D0A0e5C4F27eAD9083C756Cc2",
    abi: ~S([
      {"type": "function", "name": "deposit", "stateMutability": "payable",
       "inputs": [], "outputs": []},
      {"type": "function", "name": "withdraw", "stateMutability": "nonpayable",
       "inputs": [{"name": "wad", "type": "uint256"}], "outputs": []},
      {"type": "function", "name": "balanceOf", "stateMutability": "view",
       "inputs": [{"name": "owner", "type": "address"}],
       "outputs": [{"name": "", "type": "uint256"}]},
      {"type": "event", "name": "Deposit", "anonymous": false,
       "inputs": [{"indexed": true, "name": "dst", "type": "address"},
                  {"indexed": false, "name": "wad", "type": "uint256"}]}
    ])
end
```

The module now has one function per ABI entry. Because it has a default address, calls need no
`to:` option.

```elixir
WETH.balance_of(vitalik) |> Ethers.call!()
```

## Simulate a transaction

`Ethers.call/2` also runs state-changing functions without sending anything, which shows what a
transaction would do. This address cannot deposit a million ether, because it does not have it:

```elixir
me = "0x000000000000000000000000000000000000bEEF"
a_million_ether = Ethers.Utils.to_wei(1_000_000)

WETH.deposit() |> Ethers.call(from: me, value: a_million_ether)
```

State overrides run the same call against a modified view of the chain. Here the address has two
million ether for the duration of the call, so the deposit goes through. `deposit` returns nothing,
hence the `nil`.

```elixir
WETH.deposit()
|> Ethers.call(
  from: me,
  value: a_million_ether,
  state_overrides: %{me => %{balance: 2 * a_million_ether}}
)
```

## Events

Each contract module has an `EventFilters` module. A filter function takes the indexed arguments
of the event, and `nil` matches anything.

```elixir
WETH.EventFilters.deposit(nil)
```

`Ethers.get_logs!/2` fetches the matching events. These are the WETH deposits of the last 50
blocks:

```elixir
{:ok, block} = Ethers.current_block_number()

deposits = WETH.EventFilters.deposit(nil) |> Ethers.get_logs!(from_block: block - 50)

for %Ethers.Event{topics: [_signature, account], data: [amount]} = event <- deposits do
  %{block: event.block_number, account: account, ether: Ethers.Utils.from_wei(amount)}
end
```

## Fees

`Ethers.estimate_fees/1` estimates EIP-1559 fees from recent blocks, in wei. It is what
`Ethers.send_transaction/2` uses when you do not set the fees yourself.

```elixir
{:ok, fees} = Ethers.estimate_fees()

Map.new(fees, fn {key, wei} -> {key, "#{Float.round(wei / 1.0e9, 3)} gwei"} end)
```

## Sign and verify a message

Signing needs a signer. `Ethers.Signer.Local` signs with a private key, here a fresh random one
that only exists in this notebook.

```elixir
private_key = :crypto.strong_rand_bytes(32)
signer = [signer: Ethers.Signer.Local, signer_opts: [private_key: private_key]]

{:ok, [account]} = Ethers.Signer.Local.accounts(private_key: private_key)
account
```

```elixir
{:ok, signature} = Ethers.personal_sign("Hello from Livebook", signer)
```

`Ethers.Signature` verifies a signature from any account, including smart-contract wallets.

```elixir
Ethers.Signature.verify_message("Hello from Livebook", signature, account)
```

```elixir
Ethers.Signature.verify_message("Something else", signature, account)
```

## Where to go next

Sending a transaction works the same way as signing a message: pipe a contract function into
`Ethers.send_transaction/2` with `:from` and a signer. It needs an account with funds, so it is
not part of this notebook.

